<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Total from './lib/Total.abide'
import totalSource from './lib/Total.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="The two blocks">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>Branches</th><th>Asks</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'{#if cond}'}</code></td><td><code>{'{:else if}'}</code>, <code>{'{:else}'}</code></td><td>is it true</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'{#try}'}</code></td><td><code>{'{:catch e}'}</code>, <code>{'{:finally}'}</code></td><td>did rendering this fail</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Both are <strong>render-time</strong> structure rather than a filter over markup that
        exists anyway: the branch not taken has no nodes. And both bind each reactive read in their
        subject <strong>once for the body</strong>, so a condition mentioning a value three times is
        one subscription and one read.</p>

        <p><strong>There is no <code>{'{#switch}'}</code>.</strong> Matching one expression against
        several constants is a chain of <code>{'{:else if}'}</code>, and a second spelling for it
        would be a second thing to learn, a second lowering to keep correct and a second place a
        reader looks first. The chain reads a little longer and is the only shape there is.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title={'Whether it has landed is an `{#if}`'}>
        <p>There is no block for waiting. <code>{'{#if invoice.ready()}'}</code> is the branch that
        renders once there is a value, and <code>{'{:else}'}</code> is what shows until then. A
        probe in the condition subscribes to that probe alone, and <code>ready()</code> does not
        move for a reload in flight, so the branch stays mounted through a <code>refresh()</code>
        and its body is <strong>updated</strong> when the new value lands rather than rebuilt.</p>

        <p>Sometimes a half-arrived value is not a smaller answer but a wrong one — a calculation
        over a stream, say. <code>ready()</code> is still the branch for it: over a stream it is
        false until the <strong>close</strong>, so the <code>{'{:else}'}</code> holds the position
        until the whole thing is in.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/send-data-as-it-arrives')}>Streaming data</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title={'`{#try}` is a boundary over a region'}>
        <Example
            id="try-region"
            title={'`{#try}` — a boundary over a region, not over a line'}
            summary="An invoice converted at a rate you type. Clear the field and watch what goes with it."
            files={{
                'lib/Total.abide': totalSource,
            }}
        >
            <Total />
        </Example>

        <p>A failure <strong>replaces the whole body</strong>, not the expression that raised it.
        That is the point of drawing the boundary rather than guarding each hole: what a reader is
        left with is a message where the region was, instead of a table with one cell missing and no
        explanation for it.</p>

        <p>Where the boundary goes is therefore a decision about what stays useful without what
        failed. A <code>{'{#try}'}</code> around a page is a page that disappears; around a panel,
        it is a panel that says why.</p>

        <p>The throw is the render's own. Moved into a derived value it would reach no boundary:
        once a value has landed, a recompute that throws leaves the last one being served (2.2), and
        the failure is <code>error()</code>'s to report instead.</p>

        <p><code>{'{:finally}'}</code> runs on both paths, and it is markup rather than a callback —
        it is what stays on screen either way.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/show-a-page-when-something-fails')}>Error pages</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/repeat-markup-over-a-list')}>Lists</a> — the block for many
            rather than one</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a> — the
            probes these branch on</li>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/put-a-value-in-the-markup')}>Expressions</a> — a hole where
            a branch would be too much</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
